  "title": "Swallowtail Butterfly",
  "media_type": "movie",
  "structured_data": {},
  "metadata_synthesis": "1996 Japanese crime film. Directed by Shunji Iwai. Starring Hiroshi Mikami, Chara, Ayumi Ito. Dystopian alternate-future Japan.",
  "setting_and_subculture": "Multicultural shantytown. Sodden little rooms. Sunless skies. Xenophobic alternate-future metropolis. An indifferent universe filled with outcasts. Yen-obsessed underground.",
  "visual_tone_and_feel": "Visceral. Unbelievably cool. Dirty but beautiful. Raw heroic bloodshed. Cassette organs. Unapologetic grunge mixed with pop-singer aesthetics.",
  "media_era_tone": "90s Japanese cinema. Postmodern. Subversive. Discards the seriousness of life. Cool, kind, and rebellious.",
  "pacing_and_structure": "An epic odyssey. The big crawl of the caterpillar. Flits forward with furious legs. Sprawling narrative where every character shines. Emotional melody in the editing.",
  "atmosphere_and_mood": "Unfair. Stupid. Dirty but beautiful. Melancholic. Absurdist black hilarity. Gorgeous youth trapped in a bottle. Inherently ugly beauty.",
  "themes_and_messages": "Life beyond nationality, race, class, gender, language. Survival through relentless kindness. The world is indifferent. Metamorphosis requires pain. Forging gold from dirt.",
  "character_relationships": "Outcasts clinging together. Relentlessly kind to one another. Shared binding sadness. A band of unjustly cast-away chrysalides. Selling bodies but keeping souls.",
  "lead_character_type": "Desperate outcasts. Immigrants in a xenophobic world. Baby caterpillars crawling out of penury. Cool, kind, and resilient.",
  "story_and_plot_type": "Crime drama. Dystopian epic. An odyssey of survival. Forging gold. Bleeding for a better life. Raw bloodshed and rebellion.",
  "who_and_when": "Immigrant outcasts. Alternate-future Japanese metropolis. A timeline defined by xenophobia and yen.",
  "emotional_evocation": "Melancholic empathy. Singing sweetly and with pain. Visceral joy in an indifferent universe. Respect for the absurd."
